  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1020 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nLittle to no change of note this evening with current\ntemperatures spanning the 50s and 60s under clear skies. An\nanomalous 592dam ridge continues to dominate the weather pattern\nacross the Mississippi Valley bringing warm afternoons in the mid\n70s to lower 80s and chilly mornings in the low 40s to mid 50s. A\nvery dry airmass also remains fixed over much of the southeastern\nCONUS with current PWAT values around 0.5-0.7\", nearing the 25th\npercentile for October, across the Mid-South. Dry air will mix\ndown Sunday afternoon with decreased dewpoints in the 40s once\nagain. As such, fire weather concerns will exist Sunday. Please\ncontinue to adhere to local burn bans, properly dispose of\ncigarettes and matches, and avoid using equipment that creates\nsparks.\n\nBy midweek, southwest flow will begin to build across the\nMississippi Valley as troughing from the four corners region\nbegins to push into the upper Midwest. The forecast, however,\nwill see little to no change as a persistent dry airmass remains\nover the Middle Mississippi Valley. We could see an increase in\nhigh level cloud cover especially over our northern counties as\nembedded shortwaves move over these areas, but no precipitation\nis expected. Despite upper-level southwest flow building in, fire\nweather concerns will persist through much of the next week as\nmixing will likely result in lowered dewpoints in the 40s to\nlower 50s each afternoon.\n\nThe biggest question is, when will we see rain next. Ensemble\nmembers continue to push a pattern change further and further\nback. Latest LREF guidance is hinting at a deepening trough\nmoving into the Pacific Northwest Friday. This system looks to\ndrive the upper-level pattern across the western CONUS. By\nSaturday (Halloween), a subtropical jet begins to strengthen over\nthe aforementioned area, pushing moisture further east, and\nfurther into our neck of the woods. This may be just enough\nmoisture for us to see some very light showers next weekend,\nhowever, chances are only around 20-30%. If precipitation is able\nto sneak into the Mid-South, appreciable rainfall is not\nexpected. Maybe some good news, the latest CPC 6-10 day outlook\ndoes have us leaning above normal for precipitation heading into\nNovember. We'll play the wait and see game for now.\n\nAEH",
